# QA Bug Report (Awesome-adapted)

Use only when the user asks for a bug report, QA write-up, or reproduction doc. Does not replace the Scout/Architect/Builder/QA workflow steps.

## Severity
1. **Critical** — site/editor crash, data loss, block invalidation on live pages, security
2. **High** — major feature broken / blocks many editors or visitors
3. **Medium** — non-critical break / usability impact for some
4. **Low** — minor UI / typos / non-core
5. **Trivial** — cosmetic, minimal impact

## Structure
```
# Bug Report: [concise title]

## Description
[impact]

## Environment
- WordPress version · PHP version · Twork Builder version
- Theme + other active plugins that matter (e.g. mk-builder, WooCommerce)
- Block name (`twork/…`) and the page/template it sits on
- Device / OS / Browser · Screen (if relevant) · User role (if relevant) — no secrets/PII dumps

## Severity
[level] — [why]

## Steps to Reproduce
1. …
2. …

## Expected Behavior
…

## Actual Behavior
…

## Scope
- Editor only · Front end only · Both
- Existing posts affected? (block validation / deprecation)

## Visual Evidence
[paths / descriptions — no credential screenshots]

## Console / PHP Error Logs
[sanitize tokens/PII]

## Additional Notes
…

## Possible Fix
[optional, evidence-based]
```

## Practices
- One issue per report; factual; numbered steps; exact errors
- Include the block validation message verbatim when the bug is “this block contains unexpected or invalid content”
- No blame language; no production secrets or client PII in reports (see `security-appsec`)
